{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact Query can also be used with Preact's Suspense for Data Fetching APIs. For this, we have dedicated hooks:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../reference/functions/useSuspenseQuery.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useSuspenseQuery"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../reference/functions/useSuspenseInfiniteQuery.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useSuspenseInfiniteQuery"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../reference/functions/useSuspenseQueries.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useSuspenseQueries"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using suspense mode, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" states and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" objects are not needed and are then replaced by usage of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Suspense"},{"type":"text","value":" component from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preact/compat"},{"type":"text","value":" (including the use of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" prop and error boundaries for catching errors). Please read the "},{"type":"link","href":"#resetting-error-boundaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting Error Boundaries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" section for more information on how to set up suspense mode."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want mutations to propagate errors to the nearest error boundary (similar to queries), you can set the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" option to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" as well."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enabling suspense mode for a query:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst { data } = useSuspenseQuery({ queryKey, queryFn })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This works nicely in TypeScript, because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is guaranteed to be defined (as errors and loading states are handled by Suspense- and ErrorBoundaries)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the flip side, you therefore can't conditionally enable / disable the Query. This generally shouldn't be necessary for dependent Queries because with suspense, all your Queries inside one component are fetched in serial."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" also doesn't exist for this Query."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"throwonerror-default","children":[{"type":"text","value":"throwOnError default"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not all errors are thrown to the nearest Error Boundary per default - we're only throwing errors if there is no other data to show. That means if a Query ever successfully got data in the cache, the component will render, even if data is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stale"},{"type":"text","value":". Thus, the default for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" is:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"throwOnError: (error, query) => typeof query.state.data === 'undefined'"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since you can't change "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" (because it would allow for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" to become potentially "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"), you have to throw errors manually if you want all errors to be handled by Error Boundaries:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst { data, error, isFetching } = useSuspenseQuery({ queryKey, queryFn })\n\nif (error && !isFetching) {\n  throw error\n}\n\n// continue rendering data","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resetting-error-boundaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting Error Boundaries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether you are using "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"suspense"}]},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"throwOnError"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in your queries, you will need a way to let queries know that you want to try again when re-rendering after some error occurred."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query errors can be reset with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryErrorResetBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" component or with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQueryErrorResetBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":" hook."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using the component it will reset any query errors within the boundaries of the component:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preact does not ship an error boundary component, but one can be built with the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://preactjs.com/guide/v10/hooks/#useerrorboundary","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useErrorBoundary"}]},{"type":"text","value":" hook:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { QueryErrorResetBoundary } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\nimport { useErrorBoundary } from 'preact/hooks'\n\nfunction ErrorBoundary({ children, onReset, fallbackRender }) {\n  const [error, resetError] = useErrorBoundary()\n\n  if (error) {\n    return fallbackRender({\n      error,\n      resetErrorBoundary: () => {\n        onReset()\n        resetError()\n      },\n    })\n  }\n\n  return children\n}\n\nconst App = () => (\n  <QueryErrorResetBoundary>\n    {({ reset }) => (\n      <ErrorBoundary\n        onReset={reset}\n        fallbackRender={({ resetErrorBoundary }) => (\n          <div>\n            There was an error!\n            <button onClick={() => resetErrorBoundary()}>Try again</button>\n          </div>\n        )}\n      >\n        <Page />\n      </ErrorBoundary>\n    )}\n  </QueryErrorResetBoundary>\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using the hook it will reset any query errors within the closest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryErrorResetBoundary"},{"type":"text","value":". If there is no boundary defined it will reset them globally:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useQueryErrorResetBoundary } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst App = () => {\n  const { reset } = useQueryErrorResetBoundary()\n  return (\n    <ErrorBoundary\n      onReset={reset}\n      fallbackRender={({ resetErrorBoundary }) => (\n        <div>\n          There was an error!\n          <button onClick={() => resetErrorBoundary()}>Try again</button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    >\n      <Page />\n    </ErrorBoundary>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"fetch-on-render-vs-render-as-you-fetch","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch-on-render vs Render-as-you-fetch"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Out of the box, Preact Query in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"suspense"},{"type":"text","value":" mode works really well as a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch-on-render"}]},{"type":"text","value":" solution with no additional configuration. This means that when your components attempt to mount, they will trigger query fetching and suspend, but only once you have imported them and mounted them. If you want to take it to the next level and implement a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render-as-you-fetch"}]},{"type":"text","value":" model, we recommend implementing "},{"type":"link","href":"./prefetching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefetching"}]},{"type":"text","value":" on routing callbacks and/or user interactions events to start loading queries before they are mounted and hopefully even before you start importing or mounting their parent components."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"throwonerror-default","text":"throwOnError default","level":3},{"id":"resetting-error-boundaries","text":"Resetting Error Boundaries","level":2},{"id":"fetch-on-render-vs-render-as-you-fetch","text":"Fetch-on-render vs Render-as-you-fetch","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPreact Query can also be used with Preact's Suspense for Data Fetching APIs. For this, we have dedicated hooks:\n\n- [useSuspenseQuery](../reference/functions/useSuspenseQuery.md)\n- [useSuspenseInfiniteQuery](../reference/functions/useSuspenseInfiniteQuery.md)\n- [useSuspenseQueries](../reference/functions/useSuspenseQueries.md)\n\n\n\nWhen using suspense mode, `status` states and `error` objects are not needed and are then replaced by usage of the `Suspense` component from `preact/compat` (including the use of the `fallback` prop and error boundaries for catching errors). Please read the [Resetting Error Boundaries](#resetting-error-boundaries) section for more information on how to set up suspense mode.\n\n\n\nIf you want mutations to propagate errors to the nearest error boundary (similar to queries), you can set the `throwOnError` option to `true` as well.\n\nEnabling suspense mode for a query:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst { data } = useSuspenseQuery({ queryKey, queryFn })\n```\n\nThis works nicely in TypeScript, because `data` is guaranteed to be defined (as errors and loading states are handled by Suspense- and ErrorBoundaries).\n\nOn the flip side, you therefore can't conditionally enable / disable the Query. This generally shouldn't be necessary for dependent Queries because with suspense, all your Queries inside one component are fetched in serial.\n\n\n\n`placeholderData` also doesn't exist for this Query.\n\n\n\n### throwOnError default\n\nNot all errors are thrown to the nearest Error Boundary per default - we're only throwing errors if there is no other data to show. That means if a Query ever successfully got data in the cache, the component will render, even if data is `stale`. Thus, the default for `throwOnError` is:\n\n```\nthrowOnError: (error, query) => typeof query.state.data === 'undefined'\n```\n\nSince you can't change `throwOnError` (because it would allow for `data` to become potentially `undefined`), you have to throw errors manually if you want all errors to be handled by Error Boundaries:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useSuspenseQuery } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst { data, error, isFetching } = useSuspenseQuery({ queryKey, queryFn })\n\nif (error && !isFetching) {\n  throw error\n}\n\n// continue rendering data\n```\n\n## Resetting Error Boundaries\n\nWhether you are using **suspense** or **throwOnError** in your queries, you will need a way to let queries know that you want to try again when re-rendering after some error occurred.\n\nQuery errors can be reset with the `QueryErrorResetBoundary` component or with the `useQueryErrorResetBoundary` hook.\n\nWhen using the component it will reset any query errors within the boundaries of the component:\n\n\n\nPreact does not ship an error boundary component, but one can be built with the [`useErrorBoundary`](https://preactjs.com/guide/v10/hooks/#useerrorboundary) hook:\n\n```tsx\nimport { QueryErrorResetBoundary } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\nimport { useErrorBoundary } from 'preact/hooks'\n\nfunction ErrorBoundary({ children, onReset, fallbackRender }) {\n  const [error, resetError] = useErrorBoundary()\n\n  if (error) {\n    return fallbackRender({\n      error,\n      resetErrorBoundary: () => {\n        onReset()\n        resetError()\n      },\n    })\n  }\n\n  return children\n}\n\nconst App = () => (\n  <QueryErrorResetBoundary>\n    {({ reset }) => (\n      <ErrorBoundary\n        onReset={reset}\n        fallbackRender={({ resetErrorBoundary }) => (\n          <div>\n            There was an error!\n            <button onClick={() => resetErrorBoundary()}>Try again</button>\n          </div>\n        )}\n      >\n        <Page />\n      </ErrorBoundary>\n    )}\n  </QueryErrorResetBoundary>\n)\n```\n\n\n\nWhen using the hook it will reset any query errors within the closest `QueryErrorResetBoundary`. If there is no boundary defined it will reset them globally:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQueryErrorResetBoundary } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst App = () => {\n  const { reset } = useQueryErrorResetBoundary()\n  return (\n    <ErrorBoundary\n      onReset={reset}\n      fallbackRender={({ resetErrorBoundary }) => (\n        <div>\n          There was an error!\n          <button onClick={() => resetErrorBoundary()}>Try again</button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    >\n      <Page />\n    </ErrorBoundary>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Fetch-on-render vs Render-as-you-fetch\n\nOut of the box, Preact Query in `suspense` mode works really well as a **Fetch-on-render** solution with no additional configuration. This means that when your components attempt to mount, they will trigger query fetching and suspend, but only once you have imported them and mounted them. If you want to take it to the next level and implement a **Render-as-you-fetch** model, we recommend implementing [Prefetching](./prefetching.md) on routing callbacks and/or user interactions events to start loading queries before they are mounted and hopefully even before you start importing or mounting their parent components.\n\n\n\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: suspense\ntitle: Suspense\nref: docs/framework/react/guides/suspense.md\nreplace: { 'react-query': 'preact-query', 'React': 'Preact' }\n---\n\n[//]: # 'Info'\n\nWhen using suspense mode, `status` states and `error` objects are not needed and are then replaced by usage of the `Suspense` component from `preact/compat` (including the use of the `fallback` prop and error boundaries for catching errors). Please read the [Resetting Error Boundaries](#resetting-error-boundaries) section for more information on how to set up suspense mode.\n\n[//]: # 'Info'\n[//]: # 'PlaceholderData'\n\n`placeholderData` also doesn't exist for this Query.\n\n[//]: # 'PlaceholderData'\n[//]: # 'ExampleResetComponent'\n\nPreact does not ship an error boundary component, but one can be built with the [`useErrorBoundary`](https://preactjs.com/guide/v10/hooks/#useerrorboundary) hook:\n\n```tsx\nimport { QueryErrorResetBoundary } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\nimport { useErrorBoundary } from 'preact/hooks'\n\nfunction ErrorBoundary({ children, onReset, fallbackRender }) {\n  const [error, resetError] = useErrorBoundary()\n\n  if (error) {\n    return fallbackRender({\n      error,\n      resetErrorBoundary: () => {\n        onReset()\n        resetError()\n      },\n    })\n  }\n\n  return children\n}\n\nconst App = () => (\n  <QueryErrorResetBoundary>\n    {({ reset }) => (\n      <ErrorBoundary\n        onReset={reset}\n        fallbackRender={({ resetErrorBoundary }) => (\n          <div>\n            There was an error!\n            <button onClick={() => resetErrorBoundary()}>Try again</button>\n          </div>\n        )}\n      >\n        <Page />\n      </ErrorBoundary>\n    )}\n  </QueryErrorResetBoundary>\n)\n```\n\n[//]: # 'ExampleResetComponent'\n[//]: # 'ExampleResetHook'\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQueryErrorResetBoundary } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nconst App = () => {\n  const { reset } = useQueryErrorResetBoundary()\n  return (\n    <ErrorBoundary\n      onReset={reset}\n      fallbackRender={({ resetErrorBoundary }) => (\n        <div>\n          There was an error!\n          <button onClick={() => resetErrorBoundary()}>Try again</button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    >\n      <Page />\n    </ErrorBoundary>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'ExampleResetHook'\n[//]: # 'Streaming'\n[//]: # 'Streaming'\n"}
